"A theory of the mind is finished when it has been stated and tested. The aggregate scheme is unfinished until someone has applied it to his own experience, repeatedly, at the moment the experience occurs. Take a flash of irritation at a remark. Untrained, one experiences a single solid fact: he insulted me. With the map one can find a tightening in the chest, which is form, an unpleasant tone, which is feeling, the label insult, which is perception, an impulse to answer back and an old habit of resentment, which are formations, and the knowing of all this, which is consciousness. Nothing has been denied or suppressed. But five passing events are much harder to cling to than one fact about me, and the discourses claim that with practice the clinging stops.": "dde5bfa26715",
"Aristotles On the Soul is the first Western analysis of a living being into capacities: nutrition, perception, desire, movement, and thought. The list looks like a cousin of the Buddhas, and it is put to the opposite use. For Aristotle the capacities are powers of one substance. The soul is the form of a living body, the single principle that makes it this kind of thing and holds its activities together. To analyze the capacities is to understand the unity better. The aggregates are offered in order to show that there is no such unifying principle to be found.": "c7a2303a3d90",
"Feeling here does not mean emotion. It means the pleasant, unpleasant, or neutral flavor that comes with every experience.": "e4a84a54d8f5",
"Hume agrees that the mind is a plurality with no owner: a bundle or collection of different perceptions. His own sorting is into two kinds, impressions and their fainter copies, ideas, with impressions divided into those of sensation and those of reflection, such as desire and aversion. That is a classification by vividness and origin, and it is thin beside the Buddhist one. It has no separate place for the body, none for the pleasant or painful tone of an experience as distinct from its content, and none for the forces that shape what arises next. Hume saw that there was a bundle and did not describe how it is put together.": "e8cb382b60da",
"Marvin Minsky, a founder of artificial intelligence, argued in The Society of Mind that what we call a mind is a great number of small processes, none of them intelligent, whose interaction produces everything we credit to a self. Daniel Dennett argued in Consciousness Explained that the brain contains no place where it all comes together before an inner audience. Many streams of processing run in parallel, and what we report as our experience is whichever content wins influence for the moment. Both deny the central controller and both explain the appearance of one. In The Embodied Mind, published in 1991, the neuroscientist Francisco Varela and two colleagues set this science beside the five aggregates and proposed that the Buddhist scheme supplies what the science lacks, a disciplined way of examining experience from within.": "954ace104fa3",
"None of the five is in charge of the others, and there is no sixth.": "ff0608932023",
"One discourse defines the five by what they do, with a play on each word (Saṃyutta Nikāya 22.79). Form is so called because it is deformed by cold and heat, hunger and thirst, and the touch of insects, wind, and sun. Feeling feels pleasure, pain, and neutral. Perception perceives blue, yellow, red, and white, which is to say that it recognizes and labels. Formations, which the translator here renders choices, are whatever shapes and constructs the others, the minds intentions and habits. Consciousness cognizes sour, bitter, pungent, sweet: it is the bare awareness of an object through one of the senses. Notice that every definition is a verb. The discourse describes five kinds of happening and names no one to whom they happen.": "a359e38fc214",
"The Buddha compared them to foam, a bubble, a mirage, a banana tree, and a magic show. Each looks solid until you examine it.": "4c857d547cfe",
"The Buddha sorted everything we experience into five heaps: the body, feeling tone, recognition, intentions and habits, and awareness.": "005f38e51745",
"The Mahāyāna takes one more step, and it is aimed at the map itself. The early schools sometimes spoke as though the aggregates, unlike the person, were finally real. The Heart Sūtra opens with the bodhisattva Avalokiteśvara, who saw that the five aggregates are all empty: 照見五蘊皆空. Form is no more a self-standing thing than the chariot was. The heaps are themselves designations for conditions, and analysis can be continued through them and never arrives at a floor. No Western account of the minds parts has turned on its own categories in this way (see D8, Emptiness).": "694562941dfc",
"The Pali word khandha means a heap, a mass, or the trunk of a tree. The Buddha used it for five classes into which he sorted everything that makes up a living beings experience: form, feeling, perception, formations, and consciousness. He defines each in the plural and with the widest possible reach. Any kind of form at all—past, future, or present; internal or external; solid or subtle; inferior or superior; far or near: this is called the aggregate of form (Saṃyutta Nikāya 22.48), and so for the other four. An aggregate is therefore a category of events. It is a mistake to picture five components fitted together like the parts of an engine.": "36844bc3929d",
"The difficulty in English begins with aggregate, a word nobody uses. Heap is literal and vivid, and the five heaps is worth saying once so that listeners stop imagining components. Of the five names, feeling causes the most trouble, because in English it means emotion. Say feeling tone, and give the three values: pleasant, unpleasant, neutral. Perception should be glossed as recognition. Formations means nothing to a newcomer, and each translator has tried something different: volitional formations, fabrications, choices. It helps to say intentions and habits first and introduce the technical word afterward. Consciousness invites the listener to think of a mind or a soul, so add at once that it means the simple knowing of a sight, a sound, or a thought, and that it comes and goes with its object.": "28cb4352c515",
"The likeness ends at four places. First, the standpoint differs. The cognitive scientist describes a mechanism from outside, in the third person, and the parts he names are not things anyone can observe in himself. The aggregates are categories of what shows up in experience, chosen so that a person can check them. Second, feeling tone has a category of its own. In the Buddhist analysis the bare pleasantness or unpleasantness of an experience is the point at which craving takes hold, and Western psychology gave valence that kind of weight only recently. Third, the body is inside the scheme. Form is the first aggregate, on a level with the other four, so the analysis never divides a person into a mind and a body and never has to rejoin them. Fourth, the purpose differs. Minsky wanted to build a mind and Dennett to explain one. The Buddhas interest was in the grasping, and he analyzed the aggregates so that it could stop.": "314dc83418ea",
"The list is a tool for looking. You take whatever you believe is you and ask which heap it belongs to.": "424c18b172a1",
"The scheme was not offered as psychology for its own sake. The Buddha distinguishes the five aggregates from the five grasping aggregates, the same five when they are accompanied by defilements and fuel grasping. The reason for sorting experience in this way is given in another discourse: whatever anyone regards as a self, all regard the five grasping aggregates, or one of them (22.47). The list is meant to be exhaustive of the places where a self might be looked for. And a set of similes tells the practitioner what he will find when he looks closely: Form is like a lump of foam; feeling is like a bubble; perception seems like a mirage; choices like a banana plant; and consciousness like an illusion (22.95). A banana trunk can be peeled layer after layer, and there is no heartwood.": "14093ebecb35",
"The society-of-mind picture is the closest. It shares the three structural claims of the aggregate analysis: experience is produced by many kinds of process, the processes condition one another, and no further item supervises them. Hume shares the first and third and has little to say about the second. Aristotle shares the method of analysis by function and rejects the conclusion.": "00dd2dcd9e5b",
"When you are angry, try to find the five: the heat in the body, the unpleasantness, the label you put on the other person, the urge to strike back, and the knowing of it all.": "bfecbdf3fe1d"
}
